#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203686 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0184c705-00dd-54b0-ba83-2f467167a185" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ea5e96d2-e6ae-5bf9-86a4-01e7a019a5e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14615" :question/problem "|  | $\\bigstar$ | $\\maltese$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R$, defined over the sets $\\{3, 4, 5 \\}$ and $\\{\\bigstar, \\maltese \\}$, is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n1. $(4, \\bigstar)$\n2. $(5, \\maltese)$\n3. $(3, \\maltese)$" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over\n\n$$\nA = \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_m \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots b_n \\}\n$$\n\nof cardinalities $m$ and $n$, respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m \\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element $r_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n${1, if [math]a_i \\: R \\: b_j[/math],; 0, otherwise$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4, \\bigstar)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $\\bigstar$-column.\n- The pair $(5, \\maltese)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $5$-row and the $\\maltese$-column.\n- The pair $(3, \\maltese)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $3$-row and the $\\maltese$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d3c62a4-9293-5033-89bb-695d511f1d20" :knowledge-point/title "Building an Adjacency Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4bcec8eb-63cf-5519-b7fa-a824ee54e943" :question/math-academy-id "e-14648" :question/problem "Consider the sets $A = \\{2,4,6\\}$ and $B= \\big\\{\\{1,6\\}, \\{2,3\\}, \\mathbb{N}\\big\\}$, and the relation $R = \\{(x,y) \\in A \\times B \\::\\: x \\in y \\}$.\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $\\{1,6\\}$ | $\\{2,3\\}$ | $\\mathbb{N}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $6$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}}$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{1}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "If a relation $R$ is defined over\n\n$$\nA = \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_m \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{b_1, b_2, \\ldots b_n \\}\n$$\n\nof cardinalities $m$ and $n$, respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $r_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n${1, if [math]a_i \\: R \\: b_j[/math],; 0, otherwise$.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with $x \\in y$.\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x = 2$, then the values $y=\\{2,3\\}$ and $y= \\mathbb{N}$ satisfy $x \\in y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,\\{2,3\\})$ and $(2,\\mathbb{N})$, and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n- If $x = 4$, then the value $y = \\mathbb{N}$ satisfies $x \\in y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $\\big(4, \\mathbb{N} \\big)$, and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n- If $x = 6$, then the values $y=\\{1,6\\}$ and $y= \\mathbb{N}$ satisfy $x \\in y$. So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,\\{1,6\\})$ and $(6,\\mathbb{N})$, and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $\\{1,6\\}$ | $\\{2,3\\}$ | $\\mathbb{N}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $6$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"}] :question/id #uuid "05bbf2f9-cf55-5d11-b0d5-b647147f5b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-14649" :question/problem "Consider the following relation on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\:: \\: y = \\sin{x} \\big\\}$\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R \\subseteq A \\times B$,\n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R$, and\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R$.\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- The set of first components contains all real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$.\nHence, the domain is $\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Notice that $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R$, and it is continuous on $\\mathbb R$. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers greater than or equal to $-1$ and less than or equal to $1$:\n$\\big\\{y\\in \\mathbb R \\::\\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1\\big\\}$\nTherefore, the range is $\\{y\\in \\mathbb R\\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41ac9782-cf60-5432-8bc9-51493a97c248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Range of a Relation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9acbf7d-aced-5882-99ad-c481261c369d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200326} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a376be-5016-5bef-8c27-148564e3c357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{0}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200327} {:answer/id #uuid "63207b9c-f388-5c25-84f0-b85655a46a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200328}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3ceaa23f-1804-5224-8ea8-053f1b630108" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4ebf2d-84e9-57ac-8621-13505385ea87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200330} {:answer/id #uuid "426ea9b9-cecb-5c0b-818c-94cf32915458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200331} {:answer/id #uuid "6014913b-d7bb-57f5-a9ea-043dcf4dd40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{0}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200332}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4f261560-8924-521b-bcbc-c5320210d757" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200329}] :question/id #uuid "da7f544c-d9de-529a-84b0-118cccccae2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-213423" :question/problem "Consider the following relation on $R^{2}$.\n\n$R = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y = e^{x}}$\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?\n\nThe domain is {{field-1}}\n\nThe range is {{field-2}}"}]}
